# yosemite-from-above - machine-readable palette
#
# Format: one role=hex per line, grouped into [sections] that carry no meaning
# to a parser. Read it by line shape rather than by looking for an "=", since
# comments contain those too:
#
#   grep -E '^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=#[0-9A-Fa-f]{6}$' yosemite-from-above.colors
#
# HAND-CONFIGURED by tricolor.py. Do NOT regenerate with
# `palette.py build` - that emits the fixed two-axis seam and
# would silently discard the layout below.
#
#   tricolor.py --name yosemite-from-above --ground 261 --leaf 261 --accent 261 \
#               --detail 68 --saturation 0.35 --move reserve_1:accent
#
# Sampled from an aerial photograph of Yosemite: a river meander through a
# snowfield. 93% of the pixels are near-grey snow (image median chroma 0.011),
# the snow shadows are a pale blue at hue 261, and the dark peaty river is a
# brown at hue 60-75 covering a sliver of the frame.
#
# The picture is white, so the palette is nearly grey. ONE chrome colour, the
# shadow blue, at very low chroma (bg C 0.021, accent 0.045 - a slate, not a
# blue theme), plus the river as the DETAIL colour only:
#
#   shadow 261  bg family, the whole text ramp except text_muted/text_dim,
#               border, cursor, the accent family, accent_vivid, reserve_1
#   river   68  text_muted, text_dim, reserve_2..4
#
# Why not the river as ground: a first cut (snow-meander, retired) put the
# brown on the backgrounds because that is where the photo's only dark pixels
# are. On screen, through wezterm's 0.8 opacity over a white wallpaper, a dark
# hue-70 brown blends to khaki and reads GREEN. The ground has to be the colour
# the picture reads as, and this picture reads as cool white.
#
# Saturation 0.35: the chromatic axes' median chroma (0.025-0.034) over
# goa-cove's 0.051 gives 0.5-0.65, pulled down further on purpose so the
# terminal is as pale as the snowfield. The 0.50 build was compared side by
# side and read as a blue theme.
#
# Provenance, from `palette.py check yosemite-from-above --image <photo>`
# (OKLab dE to the nearest pixel; under 0.030 counts as a genuine sample):
# 20 of 21 roles SAMPLED; only reserve_3 (unrendered) is EXTRAPOLATED, at
# dE 0.033. The snow's shadow gradient covers the whole ladder from bg (0.008)
# to text_bright, and the river covers the two brown text roles (0.006 / 0.012).
#
# Separations: blue band 251-268 deg, 37-53 deg from mauve (304.8), 70+ from
# teal. River roles 65-75 deg, 14-21 deg from yellow (86.5): text_muted 14.4,
# reserve_2..4 15-19, all advisory, all at chroma <= 0.03 so none can read as
# a signal (the check flags no dE collision). reserve_1 was moved into the
# blue family so nvim folders match files; reserve_2..4 render nothing.
#
# Contrast: every text role and the accent family hold 4.5:1 against bg on
# paper (accent 4.53:1, text_dim 4.76:1). wezterm renders at opacity 0.8, so
# these are upper bounds.
#
# The 21 roles, in canonical order (update_palette.sh pairs them positionally):
#   bg bg_surface bg_raised bg_selection
#   text_bright text text_secondary text_muted text_dim text_hint
#   border cursor accent accent_bright accent_light accent_soft accent_vivid
#   reserve_1 reserve_2 reserve_3 reserve_4
[background]
bg=#1A1F29
bg_surface=#242B39
bg_raised=#3A3E45
bg_selection=#465065

[text]
text_bright=#F2F4F7
text=#DCE1EA
text_secondary=#ADB1B8
text_muted=#ABA093
text_dim=#978778
text_hint=#91959C

[chrome]
border=#6D7A8E
cursor=#C0CFE2
accent=#7A86A2
accent_bright=#B7C6D7
accent_light=#ADBFD5
accent_soft=#A1AABF
accent_vivid=#95ABC7

[reserve]
reserve_1=#9EAEC8
reserve_2=#B29A82
reserve_3=#A68F74
reserve_4=#614F3C
